  <h1>India’s Food Security — PDS Reforms and Nutrition Challenges</h1>
  <div><em>The Hindu | Date: 2024 • ~600 words</em></div>
  <h3>Introduction</h3><p>India’s Public Distribution System (PDS) is the world’s largest food security network, covering ~80 crore people under the National Food Security Act (NFSA), 2013. In 2024, The Hindu highlighted how reforms like digitization and portability improved efficiency, but leakages, exclusion, and nutrition gaps persist. Food security is a critical theme for UPSC, linking poverty, governance, and social justice.
________________________________________
PDS at a Glance
•	Coverage: 67% of population (75% rural, 50% urban).
•	Entitlements: 5 kg cereals per person per month at subsidized prices.
•	Free Foodgrains: PM-GKAY (2020–23) integrated with NFSA from Jan 2023, ensuring free ration.
________________________________________</p>
  <h3>Body</h3><div>________________________________________<br/>Introduction<br/><br/>Achievements<br/>1.	Digitization:<br/>o	Aadhaar-based authentication reduced ghost beneficiaries.<br/>o	100% digitization of ration cards across states.<br/>2.	Portability:<br/>o	One Nation, One Ration Card (ONORC): Enabled migrant workers to access ration anywhere.<br/>3.	Leakage Reduction:<br/>o	DBT pilots in some states reduced diversion.<br/>4.	Buffer Stock Management:<br/>o	Ensured resilience during COVID and global supply shocks.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Persistent Challenges<br/>1.	Exclusion Errors:<br/>o	~10 crore people may have been excluded due to outdated population norms (NFSA based on 2011 Census).<br/>2.	Nutrition Gaps:<br/>o	PDS dominated by rice and wheat; lacks pulses, oils, and proteins.<br/>3.	Implementation Issues:<br/>o	Aadhaar authentication failures lead to denial of foodgrains.<br/>4.	Storage & Distribution Losses:<br/>o	~30–40% food lost in transit due to poor warehousing.<br/>5.	Fiscal Burden:<br/>o	Food subsidy bill ~₹2.2 lakh crore in 2023–24.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Reform Measures<br/>•	Rice Fortification: Nationwide rollout in PDS and midday meals.<br/>•	Diversification Pilots: Some states added pulses and millets.<br/>•	Digital Innovations: GPS tracking of trucks, SMS alerts to beneficiaries.<br/>•	Community Monitoring: Gram sabhas in states like Chhattisgarh ensured better accountability.<br/>________________________________________<br/>UPSC Relevance<br/>•	GS-II (Governance): Welfare delivery, leakages, reforms.<br/>•	GS-III (Economy & Agriculture): Food security, nutrition.<br/>•	Essay:<br/>o	“Food security is not just about calories, but about dignity.”<br/>o	“India’s welfare state rests on the shoulders of the PDS.”<br/>•	PSIR Paper II: State–society contract and welfare legitimacy.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Way Forward<br/>1.	Update Beneficiary Base: Revise NFSA coverage using 2021 Census (once available).<br/>2.	Diversify Basket: Add pulses, oil, and millets to PDS for better nutrition.<br/>3.	Strengthen ONORC: Ensure portability for all migrants.<br/>4.	Leverage Technology: Expand use of AI/blockchain for supply chain tracking.<br/>5.	Fiscal Sustainability: Balance subsidies with productivity-enhancing agri reforms.<br/>________________________________________<br/>Conclusion</div>
  <h3>Conclusion</h3><p>The PDS has been India’s safety net against hunger, especially during COVID. However, to achieve true nutrition security, reforms must move beyond cereals toward diversified diets and inclusive coverage. For UPSC aspirants, PDS reforms exemplify how governance, economy, and welfare intersect in India’s development model.</p>
